 would waste a strong set. Discarding F4 is safe as winds are not helping much (F4 has 3 left, but I need Pung, unlikely). Discarding J2 or J3 risks giving someone a Pung, but they are less dangerous since many are out. B2 and B3 are safer discards as dots are less likely to complete others' hands. Considering scoring: If I keep B8, I can aim for Kong or Pung, adding points. I also need a pair; currently no pair in hand, so I need to form a pair from one of my singles. Possibly keep J2 and J3 as potential pair if I draw another J2 or J3, but that's low probability. Alternatively, discard one honor to try to pair the other. Better to discard a dot to maintain flexibility. B2 and B3 are similar; B2 has 3 left, B3 has 3 left in wall, so equal. Looking at discard history: opponents have discarded many dots and honors, so B2 and B3 may be relatively safe. I'll choose B2 as it's a simple discard.\n\nReason: Keeping the B8 triplet is crucial for potential Pung/Kong points and to maintain a meld candidate. Discarding B2 minimizes risk and preserves options to draw into Chow sequences or pairs with other tiles. The hand currently lacks a pair, so I need to develop one, and discarding a lone dot tile is less harmful than discarding an honor that could become a pair or Pung. The F2 Pung already gives 4 points, so I need at least 4 more points; aiming for All Types (6 points) is possible if I complete my hand with mixed suits and honors, which I already have components for. Discarding B2 keeps the B3 for possible Chow with B1-B2 or B4-B5, and retains honors for potential pairs.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
